<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hokuriku region</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hokuriku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hokuriku region lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, covering the prefectures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata">Niigata</a>, Toyama, Ishikawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a>. Known for heavy winter snow and rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming, it was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, whose leagues, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko-ikki</a>, ruled Kaga province for nearly a century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> much of it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda clan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hokuriku-region.jpg" alt="Hokuriku region" width="320" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brent.o.ci · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hokuriku_subregion.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
